d in the buffer once  the sample is lysed  and some kits also require the addition  of a reducing agent like better mccarthy  up ethanol or something like that  to further inhibit rna's activity  the other option would be to put your  sample into an rna stabilization reagent  there are several of those commercially  available also from macquarie nagle so  if you're interested in that you can  also go to our website and um just  search for nuclear protect uh it is  basically  a reagent and there are as i said  several of these in the market not only  from our company  uh a reagent that protects um that  infiltrates the sample and  thereby protects um the rna from  degradation so this would be  your options here  thank you mulan andreas let's come to  you for this next question  do we need any extra equipment for  magnetic bead-based  nucleic acid purification  so yeah so basically what you need is a  magnetic separator  which is also available from a diverse  set of  commercial distributors manufacturers  and then of course you need for the  automation  it is recommended to have a thermo  shaker shaking device  which is usually equipped on a bunch on  a couple of  platforms from appendoff on and you need  a gripping device  to move the plates from the magnetic  separator to the shaking device of  course you can also  do this manually then you only need a  magnetic separator and a pipette  thank you and andres let's stay with you  for this question as well  how can we affor how can we avoid  foaming  in nucleic acid purification using  silica  yeah so the deforming is a typical issue  which comes up with detergent  containing buffers during vacuum  processing  so what we have established is that in  our kit there's a certain wash buffer  with a high  concentration of ethanol but you also  can use basically  80 ethanol and we just perform an  overlay step  so basically before applying the vacuum  you just  add 150 micrometer of 80 ethanol  on top of the sample and then